SUMMARY:Silent Book Club (West Essex Chapter)
DESCRIPTION:Silent Book Club is a global community of readers with nearly 
 2\,000 chapters in 55  countries. There's no assigned reading — it's 
 bring your own book.\n\nMore than a million members gather in person\, 
 online\, and in destinations around the world to read together and swap 
 stories. All readers are welcome!\n\nHOW IT WORKS: Bring your own book. 
 Grab a seat.  Enjoy a quiet reading hour. \n\nThere are no assigned 
 readings\, no pressure to socialize. All book formats are welcome 
 (headphones for audiobooks). \n\nFrom 5:30-6pm\, participants arrive\, find 
 their seats and chat with other book lovers about what you are reading. 
 \n\nFrom 6-7pm\, please enjoy the quiet reading hour and from 7-7:30pm you 
 can continue to read or socialize. \n\nMore info: 
 https://bookclubs.com/clubs/6085923/join/676f73/
LOCATION:West Caldwell - New Book Area
ORGANIZER;CN="West Caldwell Public Library":MAILTO:programs@westcaldwell.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Book Club
UID:LibCal-10340-15968872
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/15968872
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260629T220000Z
DTEND:20260629T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260629T000000Z
SUMMARY:Between Two Worlds: Writing Workshop & Presentation
DESCRIPTION:Inspired by the book A Jewish Refugee in New York: Rivke 
 Zilberg's Journal\,  join Judith Lindbergh\, author and founder & director 
 of the Writers Circle\, as she presents her Memoir & Journaling workshop.  
 Using interactive writing exercises\, patrons will learn about these 
 similar styles of writing in a supportive\, engaging and fun way. 
 \n\nPlease bring your preferred writing tools- pen\, paper\, computer\, 
 etc. Not all participants will be able to share their writings with the 
 group. \n\nJudith Lindbergh’s award-winning new novel\, Akmaral\, about a 
 nomad woman warrior on the ancient Central Asian steppes\, was called 
 “crackling” by Publishers Weekly and “a gripping saga” by #1 NY 
 Times bestselling novelist Christina Baker Kline. Her debut novel\, The 
 Thrall’s Tale\, about three women in the first Viking Age settlement in 
 Greenland\, was an IndieBound Pick\, a Borders Original Voices Selection 
 and praised by Pulitzer Prize winners Geraldine Brooks and Robert Olen 
 Butler. Her work has appeared in numerous publications including in 
 Newsweek\, Zibby Magazine\, Next Avenue\, Writer’s Digest\, Edible 
 Jersey\, Literary Mama\, Archaeology Magazine\, Other Voices\, and UP HERE: 
 The North at the Center of the World published by University of Washington 
 Press. She has spoken at and published with the Smithsonian Institution and 
 provided expert commentary in two documentary series for The History 
 Channel. Judith received a 2024 Fellowship from the New Jersey State 
 Council on the Arts.\n\nSince 2006\, Judith has mentored adult writers 
 through the Writers Support Circle at the South Orange-Maplewood Adult 
 School. In January 2010\, she created The Writers Circle\, extending her 
 workshops to children and expanding her offerings for adults. She traces 
 her teaching approach to her background as a professional dancer and 
 actress\, and from the lessons learned from one of her greatest writing 
 mentors\, Madeleine L’Engle\, author of A Wrinkle in Time. Judith 
 believes in the uniqueness of each writer’s voice. All writers have 
 something valid to say. Judith’s approach aims to coax that pure\, honest 
 expression from each of her students. Learn more at 
 judithlindbergh.com.\n\nRead more about the beginnings of The Writers 
 Circle in this interview with Judith in Bold Journey Magazine.\n\n
LOCATION:West Caldwell - Community Meeting Room
ORGANIZER;CN="West Caldwell Public Library":MAILTO:programs@westcaldwell.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Class, Craft, Lecture, Special Event
UID:LibCal-10340-16444748
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16444748
END:VEVENT
